# GitHub `#n` is an issue or PR link

In GitHub markdown (issue bodies, PR descriptions, review comments, commit messages rendered on GitHub), `#` immediately followed by digits is reserved. GitHub turns it into a link to that issue or pull request in the current repo. `#1` becomes PR 1 even when the author meant "item 1".

Use `#n` only when you intend that link: `closes #489`, `PR #456`, "see #489".

When the number is anything else (mutiny id, proposal id, step, count, channel-style label), do not write `#` before the digits. Say the number in words, or write **hashtag** when the hash mark itself is the point.

```text
# ❌ BAD — links to issue/PR 1
Mutiny #1 is Active on Status.

# ✅ GOOD
Mutiny 1 is Active on Status.
Mutiny hashtag 1 is Active on Status.
```

Same for stacked numbers: `closes #1 #2` both autolink, and only the first binds to the keyword (see [github-pr-closes-issues.mdc](github-pr-closes-issues.mdc)).
